1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,120
A journalist told readers not to panic
about AI taking tech jobs.

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:09,080
A young graduate wrote back that telling
people not to worry was bad advice.

3
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:11,480
Her name is Kiran Maya Sheikh.

4
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:13,880
After graduating from UC Irvine

5
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:18,840
in 2025, she struggled to find her first
full-time software engineering job.

6
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:21,080
Kiran worried that companies wanted

7
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:24,280
experience while AI could take over some
junior work.

8
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:28,760
As she searched, she kept learning,
volunteering and meeting people.

9
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:33,080
By late October 2026, she told the
journalist she had started

10
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:37,400
her first full-time job as a software
engineer at Virginia Digital Service.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,080
Her story doesn't prove AI caused any
rejection.

12
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:43,360
But it leaves a question: if

13
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:47,320
entry-level opportunities shrink, where
will tomorrow's experts learn?
